  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "We define the goodness of any array BBB of length MMM as: ∑i=1M−1∣Bi+1−Bi∣\\sum_{i = 1}^{M - 1} |B_{i + 1} - B_{i}|∑i=1M−1​∣Bi+1​−Bi​∣ (Here ∣X∣|X|∣X∣ denotes the absolute value of XXX). In particular, goodness of any array of length 111 is 000.\n\nAlice and Bob have an array AAA containing NNN **distinct** elements and they play a game with it.\n\nAlice starts the game after which both the players make moves alternatively. In one move:\n\n\nA player can **delete** any subarray from AAA such that the goodness of AAA does **not** change.\nFormally, the player chooses indices LLL and RRR such that 1≤L≤R≤length(A)1 \\le L \\le R \\le \\mathrm{length}(A)1≤L≤R≤length(A) and removes the subarray [AL,AL+1,…,AR][A_L, A_{L + 1}, \\ldots, A_R][AL​,AL+1​,…,AR​] from AAA such that the goodness of AAA does not change.\n\nFor e.g. A=[3,1,2,4]A = [3, 1, 2, 4]A=[3,1,2,4], a player can select L=3,R=3L = 3, R = 3L=3,R=3, then the resulting array will be [3,1,4][3, 1, 4][3,1,4]. Initially, the goodness was ∣1−3∣+∣2−1∣+∣4−2∣=5|1-3| + |2-1| + |4-2| = 5∣1−3∣+∣2−1∣+∣4−2∣=5. After the move, the goodness is ∣1−3∣+∣4−1∣=5|1-3| + |4-1| = 5∣1−3∣+∣4−1∣=5. Here we can observe that the **goodness** does not change.\n\nThe player who is not able to make a move loses. If both the players play optimally, determine who out of Alice and Bob will win the game.\n\n\nInput Format\n\nThe first line contains a single integer TTT — the number of test cases. Then the test cases follow.\nThe first line of each test case contains an integer NNN — the size of the array AAA.\nThe second line of each test case contains NNN space-separated integers A1,A2,…,ANA_1, A_2, \\dots, A_NA1​,A2​,…,AN​ denoting the array AAA.\n\n\nOutput Format\nFor each test case, output the winner of the game if both players play optimally.\n\n\nYou may print each character of `Alice` and `Bob` in uppercase or lowercase (for example, `aLiCe`, `ALIce`, `alice` will be considered identical).",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤1051 \\leq T \\leq 10^51≤T≤105",
      "2≤N≤1052 \\le N \\le 10^52≤N≤105",
      "1≤Ai≤1091 \\le A_i \\le 10^91≤Ai​≤109",
      "All AiA_iAi​ are **distinct**.",
      "Sum of NNN over all test cases does not exceed 10610^6106."
    ],
    "inputFormat": "",
    "outputFormat": "",
    "notes": [],
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "2\n3\n1 3 2\n4\n1 2 3 4",
        "output": "Bob\nAlice",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nTest case 1: Alice can not make any moves such that the goodness of the array does not change. Therefore Bob wins.\nTest case 2: Alice can choose L=2,R=3L = 2, R = 3L=2,R=3 and remove the subarray [A2,A3][A_2, A_3][A2​,A3​]. The resulting array is [1,4][1, 4][1,4].\n\nGoodness of the array before the operation =∣2−1∣+∣3−2∣+∣4−3∣=3 = |2-1|+|3-2|+|4-3| = 3=∣2−1∣+∣3−2∣+∣4−3∣=3.\nGoodness of the array after the operation =∣4−1∣=3 = |4-1| = 3=∣4−1∣=3.\n\nThus, the array has the same goodness as before. Now Bob can not make any move. Therefore Alice wins."
      }
    ]
